    "body": "First of a stack of three (a fourth, module-typed function parameters and returns, follows). This one replaces the labeled-tuple gloss described in `docs/modules.md` with real module statics and dynamics; the next two add width subtyping and abstract type members on top.\n\n## Statics\n\n- `Sig` is a distinct, first-class type. `Typ.desugar_sig` is gone and a signature is inconsistent with a labeled tuple type in both directions. Signatures are dependent records: `free_vars`, `subst`, `normalize` and `meet` treat type members as sequential binders, and `meet` requires the same member names (exact; width arrives in the next PR). `Sig.re` exposes the member view (`Val`, `TypeManifest`).\n- A module synthesizes its signature in source order (`ModuleHelpers.module_sig_type`). Against an annotated signature, manifest type members are checked (`ModuleTypeMemberMismatch` on the `type` item) and substituted into member expectations; missing members mark the module (`ModuleMissingMembers`).\n- `M.T` resolves through the module variable's signature (`Typ.path_sig`, including `M.P.T`, module aliasing and signature aliases). The `TVarEntry` injection in the `Let` case is removed. `Dot` projects signature members.\n- `module M : S` is a signature item too (`SigModule`), so capitalized sub-modules can be declared inline. Module items report the member they declare in the cursor inspector.\n\n## Dynamics\n\n- Modules evaluate as modules. `Transition.re` steps `Module(items)` item by item with sequential scoping; an evaluated binding becomes the dynamics-only item `ModVal(x, v)`, and a module of `ModVal`s is a value. `Dot` reads bindings by name; ascribing a module value to a signature seals it. New `EvalCtx.ModuleItem` / `ModuleVal` contexts.\n- Statics still lowers a module body to a `Let`/`TyAlias` chain for type checking (reusing the `Let` and pattern machinery); elaboration refolds the checked chain back into a `Module`.\n\n## Also\n\nTyDi qualified completions read signature members; the cursor inspector and `ErrorPrint` render the new marks; `docs/modules.md` and the Modules doc slide are rewritten; tests migrated (module types are signatures, module values are modules). One skipped test pins a known gap: `{ let ? : ? }` should eventually match any member once the hole is filled.\n\n## Known gaps\n\nModule structure, roughly by how much each blocks real programs. None is a\nregression; they are what this construct does not reach yet.\n\n- **Constructors declared inside a module are not members of it** (#2243).\n  Qualified access fails and the bare name leaks into the enclosing scope, so\n  two modules declaring the same constructor make that name unusable. This\n  breaks the ordinary idiom of a type and its operations in one module.\n- **Signature items accept arbitrary patterns and then bind nothing** (#2242).\n  A tuple-pattern member is accepted, declares no members, and member access\n  on the binder then reports that the module has none.\n- **No `include` and no `open`** (#2260, #2261). Reusing a signature or\n  bringing members into scope is copy-paste, which makes larger signatures\n  repetitive.\n- **No module destructuring in patterns.** Only a name, optionally\n  signature-annotated, can bind a module; there is no way to pull several\n  members out at once.\n- **No mutually recursive modules**, and modules are values inside one\n  program rather than compilation units, so there is no import story.\n- **Module equality is not rejected statically**, only incomparable at\n  runtime.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` green \u00b7 no push before manual browser testing was the plan, so please treat this as reviewed-by-tests only.\n\n## Follow-up commits\n\n- **Module-aware projection errors, one error per differing type member.** `m.y` with no member `y` now marks the label (`ModuleMemberNotFound`, which also says when `y` is a type member used as a value) and gives the dot only a message, the design the type-level `M.Fake` already had. `M.T` with no type member `T`, a missing sub-module in `M.P.T`, and a value used as the root of a type path get module-aware marks and wording; the dot marks that said \"tuple\" say \"module or tuple\". A manifest type member that differs from the signature is reported once, on its `type` item: members are checked against the module's own definition. Slide items 11a to 11d show the messages.\n- **The function shorthand binds a member.** `let f(x) = ...` in a module body exported `x` rather than `f` (#2467, #2295): binders were read with `Pat.bound_vars`, which treats an applied pattern as a constructor application. The lowering reads a let item's binder through `FunctionSugar.binder` and annotates a shorthand at its name with the type its signature expects; the `Let` case of statics then desugars it exactly as it does a top-level shorthand.\n\nCloses #2467. Closes #2295.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n\n",
